description_en:
This undergraduate course is aimed at providing an introduction to the physics of atoms and molecules, from a modern perspective, in adequate detail, yet remaining at the undergraduate level. It serves as the basis for potential graduate studies in atomic, molecular, and optical physics, and presents a prelude to research topics and directions. 

The content of the course includes: 
1. A brief history on the development of atomic and molecular physics; 
2. Single-electron atoms 
3. Interaction of atoms with electromagnetic fields; 
4. Electric-dipole, magnetic-dipole, electric-quadrupole transitions, and their selection rules; 
5. Rabi transition, atomic clock; 
6. Two-electron atoms; 
7. Many-electron atoms, Hartree-Fock method; 
8. Diatomic molecules, Born-Oppenheimer approximation; 
9. Cold atom scattering, Feshbach resonance in cold atom research; 
10. Introduction to cold atom research.

