This course consists of two parts:
1, the fundamentals of risk management and insurance, including introduction, the international nonlife market, Chinese insurance market, introduction to risk and risk management, nature of insurance and insurance contract;
2, the fundamentals of property and liability insurance, including property insurance, marine and cargo insurance, homeowner policy, liability insurance, credit insurance, reinsurance and insurance innovation.

This course builds on Principles of Accounting and Intermediate Accounting. The course deepens students’knowledge of financial accounting and extends it to the global context, with a focus on International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS) and their comparison with Chinese Accounting Standards (CAS).

Key topics include stockholders’ equity, dilutive securities and earnings per share, financial instruments and investments, revenue recognition, leases, accounting for income taxes, accounting changes and error corrections, statement of cash flows, and full disclosure. Students will study the IFRS conceptual framework and the principles of recognition, measurement, and disclosure, while also learning how IFRS and CAS converge or differ in practice.

The course adopts lectures, case analysis, and group projects. Students will examine financial statements of international companies, compare reporting practices under IFRS and CAS, and present findings through discussions and presentations. Guest lectures from practitioners will introduce applications such as valuation and cross-border financial analysis.

Course objectives are:

To provide a systematic understanding of IFRS and its application;
To enhance comparative and critical thinking across accounting regimes;
To train students in applying international standards to complex transactions;
To expand academic and professional learning for advanced studies and international careers.

By the end of the course, students will be able to interpret, apply, and communicate financial reporting information under IFRS in global business environments, strengthening their competitiveness in multinational corporations, capital markets, and financial management.

