description_en:
As the end product of stellar evolution, compact objects form the natural laboratory for testing and discovering new physics under extreme conditions. This course will introduce the formation and physical properties of compact objects as well as the associated astronomical observations. This course is organized according to the following modules:
1.	The formation and statistical distribution of compact objects;
2.	Physical model and observational properties of white dwarfs;
3.	Neutron stars and pulsars;
4.	Black holes in astrophysical context and gravitational wave astronomy

Student participation and problem sets play an instrumental part throughout the course. The lectures will be further supplemented by final reports and presentations from students on topics of their own choice in the relevant field.

